/// \file types.mla
/// \brief Doxygen reference for MLang built-in types and keywords.
///
/// This file is documentation-only. It is used by editor tooling and to expose
/// a generated Doxygen page for the language's built-in types.
///
/// \page builtin_types Built-in Types
/// \brief Reference for primitive, generic, and sum types built into MLang.
///
/// \section builtin_types_primitive Primitive Types
/// - `void`: no value; primarily used as a function return type.
/// - `bool`: boolean truth value (`true` or `false`).
/// - `bit`: one-bit logical domain (`0` or `1`), with normal ABI byte sizing.
/// - `i8`, `i16`, `i32`, `i64`: signed integer types. The suffix is the bit
///   width, so `i8` is 8 bits (1 byte) and `i64` is 64 bits (8 bytes).
/// - `u8`, `u16`, `u32`, `u64`: unsigned integer types. The suffix is the bit
///   width, so `u8` is 8 bits (1 byte) and `u64` is 64 bits (8 bytes).
/// - `f32`, `f64`: floating-point types.
///
/// \section builtin_types_string String Types
/// - `str8`: UTF-8 string type.
/// - `str16`: UTF-16 string type.
/// - `String` is the compiler-provided wrapper namespace for `str8` helpers and
///   is not a distinct runtime type.
///
/// \section builtin_types_generic Generic Built-ins
/// - `list<T>`: growable sequence container.
/// - `array<T, N>`: fixed-capacity, list-compatible sequence. Literal
///   initializers are checked at compile time so they cannot exceed `N`.
/// - `multiarray<T, D1, ..., DN>`: one-or-more-dimensional fixed-capacity
///   array with nested brace initialization and chained indexing.
/// - `mutmultiarray<T, D1, ..., DN>`: mutable-element variant of `multiarray`;
///   declare it with `var` to enable checked indexed assignment.
/// - `map<K, V>`: associative container.
/// - `tuple<T1, T2, ...>`: fixed-size heterogeneous product type.
/// - `ptr<T>`: pointer type.
/// - `size_of(Type)` / `size_of(expr)`: ABI size query returning `i64`.
///
/// \section builtin_types_sum Sum Types
/// - `result<T, E>` with constructors `Ok<T, E>(value)` and
///   `Err<T, E>(error)`.
/// - `option<T>` with constructors `Some<T>(value)` and `None<T>()`.
///
/// \section builtin_types_inline_asm Inline Assembly Keywords
/// - `asm`
/// - `x86`
/// - `x64`
/// - `aarch64`
///
/// \section builtin_types_constraints Concepts and Constraints
/// - `concept Name<T> = Predicate;` names a compile-time boolean predicate.
/// - `generic<T> where Predicate` constrains a generic declaration.
/// - `req(value: T) { ... }` checks operations without evaluating them.
/// - `typename T;`, compound `noexcept`, and nested `where Predicate;` are
///   requirement forms; `requires` is retained for compatibility.
/// - `std::concepts` provides `same_as`, `integral`, `signed_integral`,
///   `unsigned_integral`, `floating_point`, and `arithmetic`. These are library
///   declarations and require imports, rather than being primitive types.
/// - See @ref concepts for the complete concepts guide.
///
/// \section builtin_types_notes Notes
/// - Most entries document language built-ins. Standard concept entries also
///   appear here for editor discovery; their declarations live in std/concepts.mla.
/// - The `?` operator propagates `Err` values and enriches
///   `result<_, str8>` error stacks with call-site context.
/// - `cexpr(...)` and `cexpr fn` provide explicit compile-time evaluation for
///   integer and `bool` expressions.
/// - Property attributes such as `@property(hidden)` and
///   `@property(protected)` are documented here so IDE go-to-definition can
///   jump to a stable builtin reference page.

// MLang built-in types
// This module is documentation-only and is used by editor tooling.
// It is not meant to be imported at runtime.

// @builtin void
// No value. Used as the return type for functions that do not return.

// @builtin bool
// Boolean value: true or false.

// @builtin bit
// Logical one-bit value with domain 0 or 1.
// Use `bit(expr)` to convert an integer/bool expression to `bit`.
// `size_of(bit)` follows normal ABI byte sizing even though the value domain is
// a single logical bit.

// @builtin i8
// Signed 8-bit integer. Range: -128..127.

// @builtin i16
// Signed 16-bit integer. Range: -32768..32767.

// @builtin i32
// Signed 32-bit integer. Range: -2147483648..2147483647.

// @builtin i64
// Signed 64-bit integer. Range: -9223372036854775808..9223372036854775807.

// @builtin u8
// Unsigned 8-bit integer. Range: 0..255.

// @builtin u16
// Unsigned 16-bit integer. Range: 0..65535.

// @builtin u32
// Unsigned 32-bit integer. Range: 0..4294967295.

// @builtin u64
// Unsigned 64-bit integer. Range: 0..18446744073709551615.

// @builtin f32
// 32-bit floating point (single precision).

// @builtin f64
// 64-bit floating point.

// @builtin str8
// UTF-8 string type.
// `String` is the compiler-provided wrapper namespace for str8 intrinsics
// (e.g. `String::new`, `String::with_capacity`, `String::free`).
// It is not a separate runtime type.

// @builtin str16
// UTF-16 string type.

// @builtin list
// Generic list type: list<T>.
// `list<bool>` is a normal list container, not a packed bit-vector.
// For dense one-bit storage use `std::bitset::BitSet`.

// @builtin map
// Generic map type: map<K, V>.

// @builtin_fn free_objs
/// Release a heterogeneous object pack in argument order.
///
/// `free_objs(a, b, ...)` requires every argument to expose a visible,
/// zero-argument `release()` instance method. Raw pointers and strings still
/// use `free(...)`.
// Compiler intrinsic: free_objs(objects...)

// @builtin multiarray
// Fixed-size multidimensional array: multiarray<T, D1, ..., DN>.
// Each dimension is a compile-time capacity. Initialize all dimensions with
// nested braces and access elements with chained indexes such as matrix[1][2].

// @builtin mutmultiarray
// Mutable-element multidimensional array: mutmultiarray<T, D1, ..., DN>.
// The binding must use var. Indexed and compound assignments are bounds
// checked at every dimension. get(i1, ..., iN) returns option<T>; use
// is_some()/is_none() for recoverable access or unwrap() for a checked access
// that reports its source location and aborts on an invalid index.

// @builtin tuple
// Tuple type: tuple<T1, T2, ...>.

// @builtin size_of
// Builtin size query: `size_of(Type)` or `size_of(expr)`.
// Returns the ABI size in bytes as `i64`.

// @builtin ptr
// Pointer type: ptr<T>.
// Take an address with &value and dereference with *ptr.

// @builtin result
// result<T, E> represents success (Ok) or failure (Err).
// Construct with Ok<T, E>(value) or Err<T, E>(error).
// Methods:
//   - is_ok() -> bool
//   - is_err() -> bool
//   - unwrap() -> T (warning: may panic on Err)
//   - unwrap_err() -> E
// Prefer match or is_ok/is_err checks before unwrap().
// The `?` operator propagates Err values and, for result<_, str8>, appends
// call-site context (`function:line`) to build an error stack.

// @builtin Ok
// result constructor: Ok<T, E>(value).

// @builtin Err
// result constructor: Err<T, E>(error).

// @builtin option
// option<T> represents Some(value) or None.
// Construct with Some<T>(value) or None<T>().

// @builtin enum
// Enum declarations define named integral values, optionally with an explicit
// backing type such as `u8` or `i64`.
// Enum values print as `EnumName::Variant`.
// Enums are iterable in declaration order:
//   for value in Status { ... }
//   for (i, value) in Status.enumerate() { ... }
// The enumerate form exposes a zero-based `i64` index plus the enum value.

// @builtin Some
// option constructor for present values: Some<T>(value).

// @builtin None
// option constructor for absent values: None<T>().

// @builtin property
// Property field attribute: `@property ...` or `@property(...)`.
// Synthesizes getter/setter methods for struct fields and supports options such
// as `atomic`, `mutex`, `recursive`, `hidden`, and `protected`.

// @builtin atomic
// Property option: `@property(atomic)`.
// Generates atomic load/store accessors for integer and bool fields.

// @builtin mutex
// Property option: `@property(mutex)`.
// Protects synthesized property accessors with a compiler-managed mutex.

// @builtin recursive
// Property option: `@property(mutex, recursive)`.
// Requests a recursive mutex for the synthesized property lock.

// @builtin hidden
// Property option: `@property(hidden)`.
// Hides the backing field from direct access outside methods on the declaring
// struct. Access must go through synthesized accessors or explicit methods.

// @builtin protected
// Property option: `@property(protected)`.
// Allows direct backing-field access from the declaring struct and the full
// derive chain from that base down to the lowest derived struct.

// @builtin cexpr
// Compile-time evaluation keyword.
// Use `cexpr(expr)` to require compile-time folding of an integer, floating
// point, or bool expression.
// Use `cexpr fn name(...) -> T { ... }` or
// `fn name(...) cexpr -> T { ... }` to mark a function as callable from
// compile-time evaluation contexts such as `static_assert!` and `cexpr(...)`.
// Use `cexpr if` to select a branch during compilation.

// @builtin generic
// Generic function parameter introducer for runtime and compile-time functions.
// Use `generic<T, Y>` before a function declaration, with an optional
// `where Predicate` constraint. Unary concepts can use `generic<T: Concept>`.
// Inside that function, `type_id(T)` can be compared in a `cexpr if`
// condition to dispatch by the concrete compile-time argument type.
// Use req(value: T) { ... } to check struct operations without evaluating values.

// @builtin type_id
// Compile-time type identity helper.
// Use `type_id(T) == SomeType` or `type_id(T) != SomeType` inside `cexpr`
// expressions. `SomeType` may be a primitive alias or a struct type. The result
// of a comparison is a compile-time bool, combinable with `&&` or `||`.

// @builtin asm
/// Inline assembly expression keyword.
/// Use `asm(type, "...", operands...)` for non-volatile inline assembly and
/// `asm volatile(type, "...", operands...)` when side effects must be preserved.
/// Arch-qualified forms such as `asm aarch64(...)`, `asm x64(...)`, and
/// `asm x86(...)` require the matching `--target-arch` compilation target.
/// At module scope, `asm x86("...");` emits assembly outside any function.
/// Module assembly requires an architecture qualifier and has no operands or
/// result value. It is intended for freestanding entry and platform code.

// @builtin x86
/// Inline assembly architecture qualifier for 32-bit x86 targets.
/// Use with `asm x86(...)` or `asm volatile x86(...)`.
/// Requires `--target-arch x86` at compile time.

// @builtin x64
/// Inline assembly architecture qualifier for 64-bit x86_64 targets.
/// Use with `asm x64(...)` or `asm volatile x64(...)`.
/// Requires `--target-arch x64` at compile time.

// @builtin aarch64
/// Inline assembly architecture qualifier for 64-bit Arm targets.
/// Use with `asm aarch64(...)` or `asm volatile aarch64(...)`.
/// Requires `--target-arch aarch64` at compile time.

// Language keyword reference:
// - `match` performs pattern matching for result/option and literal values.
// Example:
// let value: i32 = match maybe {
//     Some(x) => x,
//     None => 0,
// };

// @builtin pub
// Export a declaration so it can be used from another module.

// @builtin extern
// Declare a function provided by an external library, without an MLang body.

// @builtin static
// Declare a static member or associated function instead of an instance
// member.

// @builtin fn
// Declare a function: fn name(value: Type) -> ReturnType { ... }.

// @builtin concept
// Define a named compile-time boolean predicate: concept Addable<T> = req(a:
// T, b: T) { a + b; };

// @builtin req
// An unevaluated structural expression: req(value: T) { ... }. Tests simple
// expressions, types, compound result constraints, and nested where predicates
// without executing them.

// @builtin requires
// Compatibility spelling for earlier constraints and structural queries.
// Prefer where Predicate for boolean constraints and req(...) { ... } for
// structural expressions.

// @builtin where
// A compile-time boolean constraint on a generic declaration: generic<T> where
// arithmetic<T> fn twice(value: T) -> T { return value + value; }. Inside req
// { ... }, where Predicate; checks a nested boolean requirement.

// @builtin typename
// Inside req { ... }, typename T; checks that a type exists, including generic
// arity and parameter bounds. The spelling type T; is also accepted.

// @builtin noexcept
// Inside req { ... }, { expression } noexcept; rejects potentially throwing
// calls. Builtin scalar operations qualify; ordinary function and method calls
// are conservatively potentially throwing.

// @builtin impl
// Define methods for a struct or implement a nominal trait: impl Type { ... }
// or impl Trait for Type { ... }.

// @builtin trait
// Declare a nominal interface with required methods and optional super-traits.

// @builtin dyn
// Construct a trait-object type, such as dyn Trait, for runtime method
// dispatch.

// @builtin return
// Return a value from the current function, or return; from a void function.

// @builtin likely
// Mark a boolean condition or branch as expected to be true. This is an
// optimization hint; it does not change the result.

// @builtin unlikely
// Mark a boolean condition or branch as expected to be false. This is an
// optimization hint; it does not change the result.

// @builtin if
// Conditionally execute a block. Compile-time branch selection uses cexpr if.

// @builtin else
// Provide the alternate branch of an if or cexpr if expression or statement.

// @builtin try
// Execute a block with a catch handler to handle exceptions.

// @builtin catch
// Handle an exception raised from a try block.

// @builtin throw
// Raise an exception payload and unwind to a matching catch handler.

// @builtin match
// Select a branch by matching values or patterns, including enum, option, and
// result variants.

// @builtin switch
// Select a case from an integral or enum value.

// @builtin case
// Introduce a matching branch in a switch statement.

// @builtin default
// Introduce the fallback branch in a switch statement.

// @builtin for
// Iterate over a range or iterable value: for value in values { ... }.

// @builtin while
// Repeat a block while a condition is true; guarded binding forms are also
// supported.

// @builtin unsafe
// Mark a block containing operations that require explicit unsafe context,
// such as unproven raw-pointer dereferences.

// @builtin volatile
// Mark inline assembly as side-effecting: asm volatile(...).

// @builtin in
// Separate loop bindings from the range or iterable in a for statement.

// @builtin break
// Exit the innermost loop or supported breakable control-flow construct.

// @builtin continue
// Skip to the next iteration of the innermost loop.

// @builtin mod
// Declare a module dependency: mod std::concepts;

// @builtin namespace
// Group declarations under a qualified path: namespace geometry { ... }.

// @builtin use
// Import a module symbol, wildcard, or alias: use std::concepts::*;.

// @builtin as
// Give an imported symbol or module an alias: use std::concepts::same_as as
// Same;.

// @builtin alias
// Declare a type alias: alias Number = i32;.

// @builtin type
// Use type in a type import, or type T; for a structural type requirement
// inside req { ... }.

// @builtin object
// Construct an anonymous object with named fields: object { value: 42 }.

// @builtin let
// Introduce an immutable binding with an explicit or inferred type.

// @builtin var
// Introduce a mutable binding with an explicit or inferred type.

// @builtin true
// The boolean true literal.

// @builtin false
// The boolean false literal.

// @builtin array
// A fixed-capacity, list-compatible container: array<T, N>. The capacity is
// compile-time constant, and literal initializers cannot exceed it.

// @builtin struct
// Declare a named aggregate with fields and optional methods.

// @builtin field
// Declare a compact single-field wrapper for builder syntax: field Width:
// i32;.

// @builtin narrow_cast
// Perform a checked narrowing integer cast: narrow_cast<i8>(value). Range
// checks are enabled in debug builds.

// @builtin is_constructible
// Use is_constructible<T>(value) in a boolean pack fold to check whether every
// argument can construct T.

// @builtin operator
// Declare a compiler-supported user-defined literal suffix: fn operator ""
// _name(value: u64) -> u64 { ... }. Builtin integer, floating-point, and
// string signatures are supported.

// @builtin mut
// Mutable reference modifier in &mut T and &mut value. The referenced storage
// must permit mutation.

// @builtin same_as
// Standard concept same_as<T, U> is true for the same resolved type, including
// reference structure and mutability. Import std::concepts. The declaration is
// in std/concepts.mla.

// @builtin integral
// Standard concept integral<T> accepts bool, bit, and signed or unsigned 8-,
// 16-, 32-, and 64-bit integers. Import std::concepts.

// @builtin signed_integral
// Standard concept signed_integral<T> accepts i8, i16, i32, and i64. Import
// std::concepts.

// @builtin unsigned_integral
// Standard concept unsigned_integral<T> accepts u8, u16, u32, u64, bool, and
// bit. Import std::concepts.

// @builtin floating_point
// Standard concept floating_point<T> accepts f32 and f64. Import
// std::concepts.

// @builtin arithmetic
// Standard concept arithmetic<T> accepts integral<T> or floating_point<T>.
// Import std::concepts; this is a library predicate, not a new primitive type.
